# Lesson 3, new exercise: an old sequence (1NT-2NT, declined). Eight tricks needed: force out the ♦A.
title: Eight tricks in 2NT
id: L03-22
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.2
student: S
board: 54
dealer: E
vulnerable: EW

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  K62    A54    T765   Q84
  E  753    JT976  92     AT6
  S  AQ4    K83    KQJ4   932
  W  JT98   Q2     A83    KJ75

auction:
  E Pass
  S 1NT*   = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 15 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 2NT    = 8-9 points, balanced: invites game
  E Pass
  S Pass*  = Declines: a minimum 1NT
     key: 15 is the bottom of your range. Decline the invitation.
     not 3NT: Partner asked whether you have the top of your range. Did you notice that 15 is the bottom?
     not *: Partner's 2NT asks: are you at the top of your 15-17?
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner invited with 2NT. With 15, the bottom of your
  range, you declined. You are declarer, because you bid no-trump first, and
  so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 8
  spades: 3 = The ace, king and queen.
  hearts: 2 = The ace and king.
  diamonds: 3 = Three, once the ace is gone.
  clubs: 0 = None.
  remind: In 2NT you need 8 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit.
  remind: Your diamonds are missing only the ace. How many tricks will they give once it's gone?
play:
  1   W SJ  N S2  E S3  S SQ
      note: Five winners on top: three spades and two hearts. Your diamonds give three more once the ace is gone.
  2   S DK  W DA  N D5  E D2
      note: Start on diamonds right away.
  3   W ST  N SK  E S5  S S4
  4   N D6  E D9  S DQ*  W D3
      DQ key: Play your queen. It wins the trick.
      DQ not D4: Did you see that East could win this trick? Your queen wins it.
      note: Back to the diamonds.
  5   S DJ  W D8  N D7  E H6
      note: Keep going with the diamonds.
  6   S D4  W C5  N DT  E S7
      note: The opponents are out of diamonds, so even the small ones win.
  7   N H4  E H7  S HK  W H2
      note: Now take your other winners.
  8   S SA  W S8  N S6  E C6
      note: Your ♠A.
  9   S H3  W HQ  N HA  E H9
      note: That's eight tricks.
  10  N H5  E HJ  S H8  W S9
      note: The rest belong to the defense.
  11  E HT  S C2  W C7  N C4
  12  E CA  S C3  W CJ  N C8
  13  E CT  S C9  W CK  N CQ
      note: 8 tricks: 2NT made.

play recap:
  The plan: count five winners on top, then force out the ♦A to set up three
  diamond tricks.

  You stopped below game with 15 opposite 8-9, and eight tricks was just
  right.
